    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Nationalism holds that the nation and the state should be congruent: that a nation should govern itself, free from outside interference, and is the only rightful source of political power.</li><li>Scholars frequently date its beginning to the late 18th century, with the American Declaration of Independence or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>; most now see nations as socially constructed, “imagined communities” in Benedict Anderson’s phrase, not as having always existed.</li><li>It has been a feature of movements for freedom, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> to the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and it has also been used to suppress minorities and start wars, and is frequently cited as a cause of both world wars.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s national symbols were made before the English word nationalism appeared, around 1798: John Arbuthnot invented John Bull in 1712, Thomas Arne composed “<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>” in 1740, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-flag">Union Jack</a> was adopted as the national flag in 1801.</p></aside>
    </section>

